WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.480
Film Courage: Do you believe in screenplay
structure?

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:04.330
Christine Conradt, screenwriter: I do.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:07.740
I believe in the three-act structure.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:15.349
I believe in learning the rules so you can
then break the rules but there is an acceptable

00:00:15.349 --> 00:00:20.460
structure to storytelling and there is a reason
it works and I think if you can get really

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:26.570
good at that, so good that then you can manipulate
it then that serves you.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:29.750
Film Courage: What was one of the first books
on screenwriting that you read?

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:35.470
Christine: I think the Syd Field book because
it was required reading at USC film school

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:41.810
but it’s still a fantastic book and I think
I actually still have it in my library.

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:47.030
Film Courage: When you are writing dialogue
are your rules for it the same or it really

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:51.040
depends because of the character or the producer
(you know what they want)?

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:52.300
Christine: It depends.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.510
Yeah, a lot of it is what the producer wants.

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:00.270
But a lot of it is just finding the character’s
voice.

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:06.680
One of the mistakes screenwriters make is
that all the characters sound exactly alike.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:11.680
You should be able to block out the character
name and know who is talking.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:21.750
And you can learn a lot about how people speak
just by sort of listening, listening in coffee

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.930
shops by how people talk.

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:26.570
People are rarely very self-reflective.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:29.930
People rarely say exactly what they mean.

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:32.430
People rarely talk in complete sentences.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:38.640
And so the words we use choose and so when
you think about when you go to say something

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.860
there are probably a hundred different ways
you can think of to say it.

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:48.100
Why are you choosing the particular way to
say it and when you start to think like that…and

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:52.470
why your character is choosing to say something
that way and what they really don’t want

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:57.700
to reveal like why will they go to this length
but they won’t go just a little bit more

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:59.010
vulnerable.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:03.640
When you kind of understand that about your
characters then I think writing the dialogue

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:04.640
becomes easier.

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:07.390
But you have to put that thought process in
to it.

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:13.340
To know what they want to conceal and what
they want to give, I think.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:17.540
And then you can start working with the subtext,
the kind of stuff that makes all of that flavor

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:18.540
kind of come out.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:23.430
Film Courage: Would you say that the productions
or the networks that you are writing for are

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:24.690
heavy on dialogue?

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:29.030
Just because I mean the nature of women, we
tend to talk more (especially with other women)

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:32.220
that’s more…we kind of get together and
talk and it’s heavy dialogue.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:35.600
Christine: I think…well the genres tend
to be heavier on dialogue.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:42.700
I think also when you are doing a very contained,
like low-budget that has a lot to do with

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:48.520
it too because a lot of times the people are
sitting and talking, you’re in a room, you’re

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:51.140
in a house or whatever so you are sitting
and talking.

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:56.780
But I think making sure that that pacing is
good so it really doesn’t feel like people

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:58.000
explaining things.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.210
That’s kind of the death of dialogue is
when people over-explain and they don’t

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:05.860
know how to weave in that exposition kind
of nicely.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:11.170
Then it really just kind of like hangs there
that’s not good writing.

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:14.500
So hopefully there’s not too much of that
you’re watching on television when you’re

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:15.500
watching that.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:20.510
Film Courage: Well just when you said the
tone of the culture has changed, has the tone

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:23.020
of conversation and how you word it change?

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:24.120
Christine: Yeah, I think it has.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:30.060
I think that, I think that it’s less than
it used to be because I think people want

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:31.849
things to happen very quickly.

00:03:31.849 --> 00:03:36.739
Like the 2-minute video versus the 7-minute
video, so they want to get through this ad

00:03:36.739 --> 00:03:41.970
the pacing has to be much more quick than
it used to be so scenes are shorter so you

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.060
get to the point much more quickly.

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:45.689
I think that has changed over time.

